<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quchan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quchan is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a>, was assassinated by his own officers near Quchan on 20 June 1747. His death led to the rapid fragmentation of his empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quchan.jpg" alt="Quchan" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ehsan322 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quchan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Quchan is the capital of Quchan County and its Central District in Razavi Khorasan province, in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. It stands at an elevation of 1,149 metres, north of the Shah Jahan Mountains and south of the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a>. It is connected by road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a>, 125 kilometres to the south-east, and to Turkmenistan, 100 kilometres to the north. The climate is cold semi-arid. Quchan has suffered many earthquakes. Most of its people are Kurdish, of the Zafaranlu tribe, who once spoke Kurdish and now speak Khorasani Turkish.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Local historical writing connects the area with the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>. The city of Asak, near present-day Quchan, is said to be where the founder of the Parthian dynasty was crowned king, and the area is held to have been among the oldest Parthian centres. Some writers place an eternal fire there that continued into the Sasanian era. These accounts rest on traditions and speculation, and a thorough investigation of the sites has been called for.</p><p>Quchan was recognised as one of seven districts of Greater Khorasan when the first law of national division was approved in 1316, and in January 1915 it was one of the cities of the ninth province, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a> as its capital. In 1895, after an earthquake the previous year, the present city was built 12 kilometres from it, with the help of Mohammad Nasser Khan Shoa al-Dowleh, head of the Zafranlu tribe, and Russian engineers. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Shah</a>, Germans built a silo, a water source and the Atrak Bridge there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assassination-of-nader-shah">Nader Shah was assassinated</a> at Quchan in Khorasan on 20 June 1747.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The 2016 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 101,604 people in the city. It has two industrial towns, and the Quchan University of Technology and a branch of the Islamic Azad University. The town is credited with 140 historical monuments, of which 32 are on the national list.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Quchan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nader Shah was assassinated by his own officers on the night of 19–20 June 1747 at Fathabad near <b>Quchan</b>, after a plot involving commanders of his guard, including his own kinsmen, who had become convinced that his increasingly erratic and brutal behaviour threatened their lives.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
